WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.489
Three payment providers quote for one payment.

2
00:00:06.489 --> 00:00:09.536
Northpay: 30 cents, 200 milliseconds.

3
00:00:09.536 --> 00:00:12.583
Adyx: 25 cents, but 900 milliseconds.

4
00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:15.548
Kestrel: 30 cents, 120 milliseconds.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.123
Which one does the router try first?

6
00:00:18.123 --> 00:00:19.604
Finance says cheapest.

7
00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:21.893
The on-call engineer says fastest.

8
00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:24.047
Next month, someone wants a mix.

9
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.116
You could write every opinion into the router, as an if-else chain.

10
00:00:28.116 --> 00:00:33.728
Then each new one is another branch, in the same code that loops over the quotes and swaps them.

11
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:39.990
But ordering only ever asks one question, about two quotes at a time: which goes first?

12
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.312
The answer changes.

13
00:00:41.312 --> 00:00:42.913
The looping never does.

14
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.026
So split them.

15
00:00:44.026 --> 00:00:48.788
One general sort does the looping, and the answer plugs in from outside.

16
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.135
Plug in cheapest: Adyx moves to the front.

17
00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:54.450
Swap in fastest: Kestrel leads.

18
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.167
The sort never changed.

19
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.962
That plug is a strategy.

20
00:00:57.962 --> 00:01:03.612
The sort that uses it is the context, and the caller picks the strategy, at run time.

21
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.946
Now plug in cheapest, then fastest for ties.

22
00:01:06.946 --> 00:01:08.519
What order do you get?

23
00:01:08.519 --> 00:01:10.092
Pause and work it out.

24
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.908
Adyx first, at 25.

25
00:01:13.908 --> 00:01:19.755
Northpay and Kestrel tie at 30, so speed decides: Kestrel, then Northpay.

26
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:25.214
In the 1994 Design Patterns book, each plug was a class with one method.

27
00:01:25.214 --> 00:01:29.879
Since Java 8, a lambda or a method reference is the whole class.

28
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:34.134
In code, each plug is one line, in Java and in Kotlin.

29
00:01:34.134 --> 00:01:37.833
The second call chains the tie-breaker behind the first.

30
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:43.069
A fee rule has the same shape: one method, from a payment to a fee.

31
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:45.777
Java names it with a one-line interface.

32
00:01:45.777 --> 00:01:48.281
In Kotlin, a function type is enough.

33
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.955
Template Method, the other half of this lesson, has a hole too.

34
00:01:52.955 --> 00:01:56.596
A base class fixes the steps, and a subclass fills one in.

35
00:01:56.596 --> 00:01:59.985
Modern code passes that step in as a function instead.

36
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:07.515
The rule: keep the steps in one place, and pass in the one decision that varies, usually as a lambda.

37
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:09.282
A quick check.

38
00:02:09.282 --> 00:02:14.151
A fourth provider, Bluefin, quotes 25 cents and 150 milliseconds.

39
00:02:14.151 --> 00:02:17.597
With cheapest, then fastest, where does it go?

40
00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:20.089
First.

41
00:02:20.089 --> 00:02:23.313
It ties Adyx on fee, and it's faster.
